      "math_academy_id": "q-183494",
      "sequence_position": 9,
      "problem": "Find the missing values in the following table:\n\n| Sentence | Symbolic Form |\n| --- | --- |\n| {{field-1}} | $A⇒B$ |\n| {{field-2}} | $A⇒B$ |\n| {{field-3}} | $B⇒A$ |\n| {{field-4}} | $B⇒A$ |",
      "worked_solution": "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nWe have the following grammatical constructions for $S⇒N:$\n\n- $S$ is sufficient for $N$\n\n- For $N,$ it is sufficient that $S$\n\n- $N$ is necessary for $S$\n\n- For $S,$ it is necessary that $N$\n\nLet's now compare these against the entries in our table:\n\n- \"$A$ is sufficient for $B$\" means $A⇒B.$\n\n- \"For $B,$ it is sufficient that $A$\" means $A⇒B.$\n\n- \"$A$ is necessary for $B$\" means $B⇒A.$\n\n- \"For $B,$ it is necessary that $A$\" means $B⇒A.$\n\nThe completed table is shown below.\n\n| Sentence | Symbolic Form |\n| --- | --- |\n| $A\\text{ is sufficient for }B$ | $A⇒B$ |\n| $\\text{For }B,\\text{ it is sufficient that }A$ | $A⇒B$ |\n| $A\\text{ is necessary for }B$ | $B⇒A$ |\n| $\\text{For }B,\\text{ it is necessary that }A$ | $B⇒A$ |",
